    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_1_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "Mental health of clinical staff working in high-risk epidemic and pandemic health emergencies a rapid review of the evidence and living meta-analysis",
      "reason": "The title provides supplementary information about the analysis method and update status by including 'rapid review' and 'living meta-analysis', indicating both the review approach and that it is maintained as a living review."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 80
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_3",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "Importantly however, high rates of poor mental health are common in clinical staff working in acute medicine generally [3-5] and so estimating the additional impact of epidemic and pandemic response, not solely the extent of poor mental health, is also important in guiding decisions to protect staff mental health. Given that the SARS-CoV-2/COVID-19 pandemic is likely to evolve in location, course and severity over time, traditional meta-analyses are only likely to give a snapshot of the current state of the evidence.",
      "reason": "The introduction describes the current state (high baseline rates of poor mental health among clinical staff) and highlights uncertainty due to the evolving nature of the pandemic and limits of traditional meta-analyses."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "Purpose The SARS-CoV-2 / COVID-19 pandemic has raised concerns about the potential mental health impact on frontline clinical staff. However, given that poor mental health is common in acute medical staff, we aimed to estimate the additional burden of work involving high exposure to infected patients.",
      "reason": "The abstract and introduction make the importance and need for the review explicit: concerns about frontline staff mental health and the specific aim to estimate additional burden from high-exposure work."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "Recently, a meta-analysis by Kisely et al. [6] reported that healthcare workers in direct contact with affected patients had an approximately 1.71-1.74 odds ratio of reporting acute mental health problems compared to healthcare workers not in direct contact with infected patients... Living systematic reviews or meta-analyses are a new innovation that allow estimates to be updated as new evidence emerges [7, 8] but have rarely been used to date.",
      "reason": "The paper cites a recent similar meta-analysis (Kisely et al.) and explains the need for a living review/update approach, justifying the current review relative to existing work."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The review assesses mental health outcomes (prevalence and symptom scores) rather than evaluating interventions; therefore describing an intervention mechanism is not applicable."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The review does not evaluate a complex intervention or delivery context requiring a logic model; it examines mental health outcomes across epidemics/pandemics, so a logic model is not applicable."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 90
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_4",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e1",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "Purpose The SARS-CoV-2 / COVID-19 pandemic has raised concerns about the potential mental health impact on frontline clinical staff. However, given that poor mental health is common in acute medical staff, we aimed to estimate the additional burden of work involving high exposure to infected patients.",
      "reason": "The review objective is explicitly stated (to estimate the additional burden of high-exposure work on mental health), but it is not framed using a formal question-formulation framework (e.g., PICO or another structured framework), so the requirement for use of a relevant question-formulation framework is not fully met."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e2",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "Consequently, we report a rapid review and meta-analysis that forms the basis of a living meta-analysis of the mental health of clinical staff dealing with epidemics and pandemics of high-risk infectious diseases, including studies from ... to understand the potential impact on mental health and to inform policy on supporting staff during the current COVID-19 pandemic.",
      "reason": "The review investigates the impact of exposure on mental health (observational comparisons of high- vs low-exposure work) rather than effects of interventions; therefore planned intervention comparisons using PICO are not applicable."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 90
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_5",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "Articles were included if they were peer-reviewed articles published in English or Spanish (the languages of the authors) that reported mental health outcomes in clinical staff managing high-risk infectious disease outbreaks. We considered studies on all healthcare staff in all settings as relevant. One article in Chinese with numeric results reported in the English-language abstract was also included. Studies of methodologies including quantitative studies, qualitative studies, and anecdotal accounts, reporting original results were included.",
      "reason": "The manuscript specifies study characteristics used for eligibility: populations (clinical/healthcare staff managing high-risk infectious disease outbreaks), settings (all settings), eligible study designs/methodologies (quantitative, qualitative, anecdotal reports with original results), and includes an exception for one Chinese article; these elements directly inform eligibility."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e2",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "Articles were included if they were peer-reviewed articles published in English or Spanish (the languages of the authors) that reported mental health outcomes in clinical staff managing high-risk infectious disease outbreaks. We also searched pre-print servers MedRXviv and SSRN for pre-prints relating to COVID-19.",
      "reason": "The review specifies language eligibility (English or Spanish) and restricts included articles to peer-reviewed articles (publication status). However, dissemination year eligibility is not specified, so the criterion is only partially addressed."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_9",
      "quote": "More studies reported results from the analysis of mental health measures than reported sufficient detail (prevalence, mean scores etc.) to allow them to be included in the numeric assessment of results in this review.",
      "reason": "The manuscript distinguishes studies that lacked mental health content or sufficient reported outcome detail (i.e. outcomes not reported in usable form) from those measuring outcomes, and the study flow figure (Fig.1) lists exclusion reasons such as 'with no mental health content', indicating differentiation between not measured versus not reported."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e4",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "We defined high and low exposures as direct contact with infected patients, or work in wards where direct contact was considered highly likely (e.g. critical care, designated treatment wards, emergency departments) compared to clinicians working without direct contact or work in hospital areas where direct contact was unlikely.",
      "reason": "The review specifies the groups used in syntheses (high-exposure vs low-exposure) and links these groups to the comparisons of interest; meta-analytic results (e.g. Table 4) present SMDs comparing these exposure groups."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e5",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "Articles were included if they were peer-reviewed articles published in English or Spanish (the languages of the authors) ... One article in Chinese with numeric results reported in the English-language abstract was also included.",
      "reason": "The manuscript provides a rationale for the language restriction by noting included languages are 'the languages of the authors', showing an explicit reason for that notable eligibility restriction."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "85"
}

